From 6a50d57b0b838ca3c074ecf287cf1ad9a30b9247 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: adbtrr_work4_mq_assert <>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 07:02:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] os/kernel/mqueue: add reproducer test for ASSERT(btcb) in
 mq_doreceive()

mq_doreceive() evaluates its "if (msgq->nwaitnotfull > 0)" guard outside
the critical section that then searches g_waitingformqnotfull, and it is
called by mq_receive()/mq_timedreceive() only after both
leave_critical_section() and sched_unlock().  The guard therefore runs
with interrupts and pre-emption enabled.

A mq_timedsend() timeout expiring in the timer interrupt runs
mq_sndtimeout() -> mq_waitirq(), which clears nwaitnotfull and unlinks the
waiter.  When that lands inside the window, the search finds nothing and
the always-compiled-in ASSERT(btcb) panics the kernel.

Add a negative test case that drives exactly that ordering:

  apps/examples/testcase/le_tc/kernel/tc_mqueue_assert.c
    blocker thread - mq_timedsend() with a one tick deadline, so it parks
                     in mq_waitsend() with nwaitnotfull == 1 and a
                     watchdog armed
    receiver       - tight mq_receive() loop crossing the guard once per
                     iteration

The test panics the board by design, so CONFIG_TC_KERNEL_MQUEUE_ASSERT
defaults to n and is not selected by CONFIG_TC_KN_ALL.

On stock sources the hit is statistical, typically a few seconds.
CONFIG_MQ_ASSERT_RACE_WINDOW_US stretches the window with up_udelay() so
it can be reproduced deterministically; it defaults to 0 and compiles away
entirely, leaving production builds unchanged.

The companion ASSERT(btcb) in mq_dosend() (mq_sndinternal.c) is not
reachable this way: there the guard and the list walk share a single
enter_critical_section(), so the invariant cannot be broken between them.
